WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.879 align:start position:0%
 
you  okay so in the final scene of Kill Bill  Vol - by Quentin Tarantino the eponymous  villain bill tranquilizes the hero and  the narrative to deliver a short  monologue on his deep affection for  comic book superheroes namely Superman  the Man of Steel he says stands apart  from other heroes like Batman spider-man  etc in that unlike those others who have  to Don the costume that they may become  the hero  Superman wakes as Superman and must  instead Don the costume of Clark Kent  the mild-mannered reporter from The  Daily Planet who bill believes is  Superman's critique on the human race  now I don't know if Tarantino believes  this or it's just Bill who does but  being a huge fan of Superman I have to  say that I can't agree with this  characterization of the last Son of  Krypton not too long ago when I didn't  have to pay for rent for utilities or  groceries or car insurance I used to  read comic books every Wednesday I'd go  to the local comic book store and pick  up new editions of the DC monthlies  mainly the for Superman titles which  were at that time still running Action  Comics Superman The Adventures of  Superman and the Man of Steel I was the  only one of my friends who read comics  regularly and so it was sort of a thrill  going there every Wednesday because it  was my first real sense of a community  in which everyone was connected by their  love of something in this case comics  and I've been a huge fan of communities  like that ever since  hence anyway I became fascinated with  Superman probably for the same reason  that a lot of young boys do they get  lost in that fantasy of unlimited  physical power Superman could fly and it  is an immutable fact of the universe  that every boy everywhere knows exactly  which combination of muscles to clench  in order to take off into the sky or  shoot fire from your eyes or see through  walls Superman could do all those things  he could fly SuperSpeed hear cries for  help at great distances he could pick up  cars and hurl them like baseballs and  how cruel were the stupid stupid laws of  physics for preventing all us regular  humans from doing such things when it's  so easy to imagine I don't think that  those fantasies ever leave us but as I  grew older the story of Superman began  to captivate me for different reasons I  think when we're young we feel that to  achieve those feats of strength would  solve a lot of our problems if I could  only fly if I could be the one who flies  that I could be special and important in  all those abstract things but as we make  our way through adolescence  the complexities of the inner life begin  to muddy those waters the pen with which  you had to write your own identity gets  put into your hand and you realize that  no feat of strength or speed will help  you with that task for Superman the task  would be even more difficult his  strength is speed his ability to fly  serve only to neutralize the physical so  that the intellectual and emotional  problems that plagued all of us how to  love how to be loved how to know who we  are what we should want how we should  act why we're here appear only in  starker relief all his incredible power  becomes an equally incredible  helplessness Clark Kent can't wake up  free from these things just as neither  you nor I can ignore the personal and  ethical dilemmas that define us and it's  ironic that in the comics his feelings  of being an alien from another planet of  not fitting in are the cause of so much  sorrow because it is these same feelings  of sorrow for not fitting in for not  belonging that make the rest of us human  Clark Kent is Clark Kent he puts on the  costume to become Superman it is as  godly and perfect as we would have  others believe that we are he saves the  world from extraterrestrial existential  threats from villains of every shape  size and hairstyle yet he is as impotent  as the rest of us when it comes to  saving himself from the bumbling  stuttering forever struggling condition  of life and maybe Clark Kent as Clark  Kent's critique on the human race but to  say that is simply to say that we are  all continuously critiquing the human  race when we for example make total  fools of ourselves in front of the Lois  Lane's of our lives and as beautiful as  Lois seems to Clark we know that she  herself feels inadequate in the presence  of Superman the great hero who is after  all only the same Clark Kent who's  invincible needs  can't seem to keep from going weak in  the presence of her kryptonite eyes some  critique  yeah

